## 4. V3 design direction — "QUIET PRESS / SOUND-FIRST"

**One-line concept:** the same midnight gallery wall as V2, but the lights came up and
the clutter came down — one confident poster per view, generous black space, a single
pink signal, and Estrella tags used like a rare signature instead of wallpaper.
Discovery as *taste*, not noise.

**The one thing someone remembers:** a single oversized **Smiley Sans** headline sitting
in a sea of black, with **one** Estrella graffiti accent and **one** pink mark — and the
restraint that makes it feel expensive. Where V2 shouted on every element, V3 lets *one*
element shout per screen.

### What "cleaner / less going on" means concretely (the V3 contract)

Remove or dial way down vs V2:
- ❌ Screen-print **mis-registration ghosting** on type (the offset color duplicates) —
  remove from body/UI; allow at most on the single hero wordmark, subtle.
- ❌ **Halftone dot fields** behind everything → at most one faint field, one screen.
- ❌ **Marquee tickers** between sections → cut, or one slow quiet one max.
- ❌ **Sticker rotations / tape / torn-poster** on every card → cards sit straight and
  calm; rotation reserved for ≤1 intentional accent per screen.
- ❌ Multiple loud colors per viewport → **one signal (pink) per view**, blue/purple rare.
- ✅ Keep: black canvas, sharp corners (radius 0), a *faint* grain, big type, hairlines.
- ✅ Add: **negative space as a design element** — let things breathe. Fewer, larger,
  more deliberate moves. Strong typographic hierarchy doing the work instead of effects.

Rule of thumb: **if V2's instinct was "add one more texture," V3's instinct is "remove
one more thing." When in doubt, take it out.**

### Type system (3 roles, matching the brief)

| Role | Font | CSS var | Used for |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Tag / signature** | behance Estrella | `--font-burst` | the wordmark and **one** big section stamp or number per screen. Rare and large — a signature, not a texture. Never set sentences in it. |
| **Accent / display** | Smiley Sans (得意黑) | `--font-accent` | headlines, big UI numbers, buttons, nav, labels, track titles. The workhorse loud voice — but used on fewer, bigger things in V3. |
| **Body / read** | Helvetica | `--font-body` | paragraphs, metadata, captions, descriptions. Neutral, clean, Swiss — the calm that makes the loud parts land. |

> Note: `--font-burst` keeps its variable name for continuity, but the *font behind it
> changes* from V2's Permanent Marker placeholder → real **Estrella.otf**. "Burst" is
> just the internal slot name for the graffiti-tag role.

### Color tokens (exact hex from the brief)

```
--blue:   #5b75f4   /* supporting ink — section blocks, step accents, structure */
--black:  #000000   /* base canvas (the dominant ground) */
--white:  #FFFFFF   /* primary text on black, "paper" inserts */
--violet: #b095f8   /* supporting ink — soft accent, hover, catalog badges */
--pink:   #E6628E   /* PRIMARY SIGNAL — CTAs, active state, the put-on mark, the rec count */
